    internalLinks: [{ anchor: "Sit-Stand Desks", href: "/products" }, { anchor: "Sit-Stand Benefits", href: "/blog/sit-stand-desk-productivity-benefits" }],
    content: `
<p class="lead">Standing desks only deliver their promised health benefits when used with correct posture and appropriate standing duration. Poor standing posture — swaying hips, locked knees, forward leaning, or excessive spinal extension — can create musculoskeletal problems different from but equivalent in severity to those caused by poor sitting posture. Understanding how to stand correctly at a workstation is as important as the desk itself.</p>

<h2>Correct Standing Desk Height</h2>
<p>The correct standing desk height places the work surface at elbow height with the arms hanging relaxed at the sides — for most adults, 95–110cm above floor level. At this height, keyboard and mouse use keeps the forearms parallel to the floor, elbows at approximately 90°, and shoulders in a neutral, non-elevated position. When adjusting from sitting to standing, re-set the desk height — the correct sitting and standing heights are different and should ideally be saved as separate memory positions.</p>

<h2>Foot and Weight Distribution</h2>
<p>Correct standing posture distributes weight evenly across both feet, with feet shoulder-width apart and facing forward. Common poor patterns include: shifting all weight to one leg (creating lateral pelvic tilt), standing with weight predominantly on the heels (causing lower back extension), or crossing feet (destabilising the base of support). An anti-fatigue mat's slight surface compliance naturally encourages healthy weight distribution through micro-movement.</p>

<h2>Core and Spinal Alignment</h2>
<p>Correct standing posture maintains the spine's natural S-curve: gentle inward curve (lordosis) in the lower back, gentle outward curve (kyphosis) in the mid-back, and neutral position in the neck. Avoid the common compensatory patterns of excessive lordosis (lower back arched forward) or forward trunk lean that develops with fatigue. If these compensatory patterns appear, it's a signal to transition to sitting — fatigue management is better than persistence in poor posture.</p>

<h2>Screen Positioning Adjustment</h2>
<p>Monitor arms are essential for sit-stand desks — the monitor must be repositioned when transitioning between sitting and standing heights. In standing position, the top of the screen should be at or slightly below eye level, with the screen distance and angle matching the same parameters as for seated use. Without a monitor arm, users typically use the screen in a suboptimal position for one or both postures.</p>

<h2>Duration and Frequency</h2>
<p>The evidence-based recommendation for sit-stand desk use: alternate between sitting and standing every 20–30 minutes, targeting approximately 2–4 hours of standing per working day. Continuous standing for extended periods creates lower limb fatigue, foot discomfort, and varicose vein risk. The key word in "sit-stand desk" is the alternation — the health benefit comes from postural variety, not from standing itself.</p>

<div class="cta-block">
<h3>Get the Right Sit-Stand Setup</h3>
<p>The Corporate Desk supplies height-adjustable desks, anti-fatigue mats, and monitor arms as complete ergonomic workstation packages.</p>
</div>
`,
