    content: `
<p class="lead">The shift to hybrid and remote work has moved significant ergonomic risk from controlled, assessed office environments to thousands of individual home setups — kitchen tables, lounge chairs, spare room improvisation, and laptop-only configurations. For Australian employers, addressing WFH ergonomics is now both a Work Health and Safety Act obligation (duty of care extends to working from home) and a practical workforce health priority.</p>

<h2>The Employer's WHS Obligation</h2>
<p>Australian work health and safety legislation imposes a duty of care on employers that applies to home-based work arrangements. Organisations should have a WFH ergonomic policy that includes: minimum workstation standards for remote workers, a self-assessment tool that employees complete for their home setup, a funding mechanism for necessary ergonomic equipment, and a process for reporting and addressing ergonomic concerns. Safe Work Australia has published guidance materials on managing WFH health and safety risks.</p>

<h2>The Priority List for Home Office Ergonomics</h2>
<p>For remote workers building or improving a home office setup, the ergonomic priority order:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>A proper desk:</strong> Working from a kitchen table or other non-desk surface is significantly worse ergonomically than a purpose-built desk. A fixed-height quality desk ($250–$600) represents the highest-return foundation investment. A height-adjustable desk ($600–$1,500) is the premium choice with documented health benefits.</li>
<li><strong>An ergonomic chair:</strong> Lounge chairs, dining chairs, and bean bags are ergonomically inadequate for sustained desk work. A commercial-grade ergonomic chair with lumbar support and height adjustment ($400–$1,200) is the most health-critical single purchase.</li>
<li><strong>External monitor:</strong> Laptop-only work positions the screen too low and creates the neck flexion that causes neck and back pain. An external monitor at correct height transforms the ergonomic situation. A monitor arm is strongly recommended.</li>
<li><strong>External keyboard and mouse:</strong> Enable the laptop screen to be raised to correct height (on a stand or arm) while maintaining keyboard at elbow height.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Employer Equipment Provision Programs</h2>
<p>Many Australian organisations now provide home office equipment allowances or loan programs for remote workers. Approaches include: a one-time allowance ($500–$1,500) for remote workers to purchase their own equipment, a company-supplied equipment kit (chair, monitor, keyboard, mouse) that remains company property and returns with the employee, or a hybrid approach providing specific critical items (ergonomic chair) with allowance for supplementary purchases.</p>

<h2>Home Office Self-Assessment</h2>
<p>Remote workers can conduct a self-assessment using Safe Work Australia's online home office ergonomic checklist. Key checks: Can you adjust your chair height so feet rest flat on the floor? Is your monitor at eye level? Are your forearms parallel to the floor when typing? Do you take breaks every 60–90 minutes? Completing this assessment and addressing identified gaps significantly reduces injury risk.</p>

<div class="cta-block">
<h3>Equip Your Remote Workers Properly</h3>
<p>The Corporate Desk supplies ergonomic home office furniture for businesses supporting remote teams. Request a quote.</p>
</div>
`,
