    content: `
<p class="lead">Ergonomic keyboards and mice address the repetitive strain risks created by conventional input devices through modifications to hand, wrist, and forearm position. For high-volume keyboard and mouse users — those spending 4+ hours daily on these devices — upgrading to ergonomic input devices is among the highest-impact ergonomic interventions available, addressing the primary mechanical cause of office-related RSI.</p>

<h2>Ergonomic Keyboard Types</h2>
<p>The commercial ergonomic keyboard market offers several design approaches:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Split keyboards:</strong> The keyboard is divided into two halves that can be positioned at shoulder-width, eliminating the ulnar deviation (inward wrist bend) of conventional keyboards. Requires adaptation period of 1–4 weeks for touch typists.</li>
<li><strong>Contoured/tented keyboards:</strong> The keyboard is arched in the centre (tented), tilting both halves inward and reducing forearm pronation — addressing the rotational component of conventional keyboard use.</li>
<li><strong>Compact keyboards:</strong> Keyboards without numeric keypads position the mouse closer to the body centreline, reducing shoulder reach distance. Simple to adopt, significant shoulder strain reduction for mouse users.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Ergonomic Mouse Types</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Vertical mice:</strong> Position the hand in a handshake orientation (thumb up), eliminating forearm pronation. Reduces forearm muscle activity by 40–60% compared to standard mice according to EMG research. Recommended for users with forearm or wrist RSI symptoms.</li>
<li><strong>Trackballs:</strong> The pointer is controlled by thumb or finger rotation of a ball, eliminating wrist and arm movement from pointing. Reduces cumulative joint and tissue loading from mouse movement — beneficial for users with shoulder or repetition-related conditions.</li>
<li><strong>Pen tablets:</strong> Used with a stylus in a natural grip position. Suitable for design-heavy roles; less efficient for general office navigation but excellent for creative and technical users.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Transition and Adaptation</h2>
<p>Ergonomic input devices typically require an adaptation period of 1–4 weeks during which typing speed and mouse precision may temporarily reduce. This is normal and resolves as the user develops new movement patterns. Adaptation is typically faster for users with established touch-typing skills. Implementing ergonomic devices during a period of lower workload (annual leave return, new project commencement) reduces the productivity impact of the adaptation period.</p>

<h2>When to Recommend Ergonomic Input Devices</h2>
<p>Routine recommendation of ergonomic input devices is appropriate for: any employee reporting upper limb RSI symptoms, roles with keyboard use exceeding 4 hours daily, roles with intensive mouse use (designers, analysts, data entry roles), and as a standard specification for new workstations in high-risk roles. The cost ($100–$400 per device pair) is negligible relative to the potential WorkCover cost of a managed RSI claim.</p>

<div class="cta-block">
<h3>Complete Your Ergonomic Workstation</h3>
<p>Talk to The Corporate Desk about a complete ergonomic workstation specification including input device recommendations.</p>
</div>
`,
