    content: `
<p class="lead">Pregnancy creates significant and progressive changes in posture, body dimensions, centre of gravity, and physical tolerance for sustained sitting that require targeted ergonomic workstation adjustments. Proactive accommodation supports both the comfort and safety of pregnant workers while meeting employers' WHS obligations to manage known risks in the workplace.</p>

<h2>Postural Changes and Ergonomic Impact</h2>
<p>Pregnancy creates several ergonomic challenges: the progressive shift in centre of gravity forward increases lumbar lordosis and lower back strain; the growing abdomen changes the comfortable relationship between seat, desk, and body; round ligament discomfort and pelvic girdle pain affect mobility and sitting tolerance; and increased body mass increases loading on joints and weight-bearing surfaces. These changes accelerate through the second and third trimesters and typically require progressive adjustment.</p>

<h2>Chair Adjustments for Pregnancy</h2>
<p>Key chair adjustments during pregnancy: seat height may need to be increased slightly as the abdomen grows (bringing the knees below hip level reduces pelvic compression); seat pan depth may need reduction as the abdomen prevents comfortable contact with the backrest; lumbar support height should track the changing lumbar curve. Chairs with limited adjustment range may need temporary replacement with more adjustable alternatives.</p>

<h2>Desk Height and Access</h2>
<p>Height-adjustable desks are particularly valuable during pregnancy — the ability to adjust desk height to the changing body dimension and to alternate standing/sitting reduces sustained pressure on the pelvic floor and lumbar spine. In second and third trimesters, working at a slightly raised seated position with the desk adjusted upward can significantly reduce abdominal pressure during desk work.</p>

<h2>Break Schedules</h2>
<p>Regular movement breaks are critically important during pregnancy to prevent: lower limb oedema (fluid retention), deep vein thrombosis risk (elevated during pregnancy), sacroiliac joint discomfort from sustained postures, and pelvic floor pressure. Evidence suggests pregnant office workers benefit from a movement break every 30–45 minutes in the third trimester — shorter intervals than the standard 60–90 minute recommendation for non-pregnant workers.</p>

<h2>Employer Obligations</h2>
<p>Under Australian WHS legislation and anti-discrimination legislation, employers are required to make reasonable adjustments to workstations and work arrangements for pregnant employees. This includes: conducting a workstation assessment on request, providing requested ergonomic equipment adjustments, and modifying break schedules as medically indicated. Failure to make reasonable accommodation creates legal exposure under both WHS and sex discrimination legislation.</p>

<div class="cta-block">
<h3>Ensure Your Workstations Accommodate All Users</h3>
<p>The Corporate Desk can advise on adjustable furniture solutions for diverse workforce needs. Contact us for a consultation.</p>
</div>
`,
