    internalLinks: [{ anchor: "Workplace Strategy", href: "/workplace-strategy" }, { anchor: "Free Office Layout Plan", href: "/free-office-layout-plan" }],
    content: `
<p class="lead">Accessible office design is both a legal obligation under Australia's Disability Discrimination Act 1992 and a genuine business opportunity. Workplaces designed for accessibility benefit all users (the universal design principle), enable organisations to attract talent from the broadest possible pool, and demonstrate the inclusive values that increasingly distinguish high-performing organisations.</p>

<h2>Legal Framework</h2>
<p>The Disability Discrimination Act 1992 (DDA) prohibits discrimination against people with disability in employment, including in the provision of workplace facilities. The Building Code of Australia and Australian Standard AS 1428 set specific accessibility requirements for commercial buildings. When undertaking fitouts, organisations must ensure that new work areas meet these accessibility standards — retrofitting accessibility after the fact is significantly more expensive than incorporating it from the outset.</p>

<h2>Accessible Workstation Design</h2>
<p>For wheelchair users, workstations require: 900mm minimum width at desk surface, knee clearance of 730mm minimum height and 500mm depth, height-adjustable desk to accommodate both wheelchair and ambulatory use, clear floor circulation space of 1500mm (1800mm preferred) for wheelchair turning. Height-adjustable desks are strongly recommended for wheelchair user workstations — they accommodate the specific ergonomic needs of each individual user rather than requiring bespoke custom furniture.</p>

<h2>Circulation and Access</h2>
<p>Accessible office design requires: minimum 1200mm primary circulation corridor width (1500mm preferred), 900mm minimum door clear opening width, level changes managed via ramps (1:14 maximum gradient) or platform lifts, and accessible bathroom facilities within reasonable travel distance from work areas. Furniture placement must preserve these circulation clearances — avoid ad hoc furniture placement that blocks accessible routes.</p>

<h2>Adjustable Furniture for All</h2>
<p>Universal design in office furniture means specifying adjustable furniture that can accommodate the widest possible range of users without requiring special furniture for specific individuals. Height-adjustable desks, chairs with full adjustment ranges, monitor arms, and adjustable keyboard trays serve users of diverse body dimensions, abilities, and health conditions without creating segregated furniture arrangements.</p>

<h2>Reasonable Adjustments for Individual Employees</h2>
<p>Beyond general accessibility standards, the DDA requires employers to make reasonable adjustments for specific employees with disability when requested. Reasonable adjustments for office workers may include: specific ergonomic chair models, specialised input devices, screen magnification or colour adjustment software, private workspace for employees with conditions requiring privacy or reduced sensory stimulation, and modified break schedules. The cost of reasonable adjustments is typically modest — and the cost of failing to provide them can include DDA claims.</p>

<div class="cta-block">
<h3>Design an Inclusive Workplace</h3>
<p>The Corporate Desk can advise on accessible and inclusive office design. Book a workplace strategy consultation.</p>
</div>
`,
