    excerpt: "Ergonomic workplace modifications are a cornerstone of effective return-to-work programs for employees with musculoskeletal injuries. Well-executed ergonomic support enables earlier, more sustainable return to full duties with lower re-injury risk.",
    tags: ["return to work", "WorkCover", "injury management", "ergonomics"],
    internalLinks: [{ anchor: "Ergonomic Office Products", href: "/products" }, { anchor: "Ergonomic Assessment Guide", href: "/blog/workstation-ergonomics-assessment" }],
    content: `
<p class="lead">Ergonomic workplace modifications are a cornerstone of effective return-to-work programs for employees who have sustained musculoskeletal injuries — whether from workplace or non-workplace causes. Well-executed ergonomic support enables earlier, more sustainable return to productive duties with significantly lower re-injury risk than the unmodified work environment that originally contributed to or failed to accommodate the condition.</p>

<h2>The Employer's Return-to-Work Obligations</h2>
<p>Under Australian WorkCover legislation (varying by state but broadly consistent in principle), employers with 30+ employees must have a return-to-work program and appoint a designated return-to-work coordinator. These programs must include provision for workplace modifications where medically indicated. For musculoskeletal conditions, ergonomic workstation modification is the primary and most frequently indicated workplace accommodation.</p>

<h2>Assessment at Return</h2>
<p>Every return-to-work from a musculoskeletal condition should begin with a workstation assessment by a qualified ergonomist (internal or external). The assessment should: identify any workstation factors that may have contributed to the original injury, recommend specific modifications appropriate to the employee's functional capacity and medical restrictions, and establish a monitoring schedule for the return period. The assessment report provides the basis for both employer modifications and the treating physiotherapist's advice.</p>

<h2>Common RTW Ergonomic Modifications</h2>
<p>Modifications frequently implemented in RTW programs:</p>
<ul>
<li>Height-adjustable desk provision for employees with lower back conditions (enabling posture variation)</li>
<li>Ergonomic chair upgrade with specific adjustment features matching the individual's requirements</li>
<li>Reduced workstation setup for upper limb conditions (compact keyboard, vertical mouse, forearm support)</li>
<li>Document holder for cervical spine conditions (reducing neck rotation for copy work)</li>
<li>Anti-fatigue mat for lower limb and foot conditions requiring standing periods</li>
<li>Footrest for users whose feet don't reach the floor at correct chair height</li>
</ul>

<h2>Graduated Return Strategy</h2>
<p>Ergonomic modifications support but do not replace a graduated return strategy that manages hours and duties in progressive steps rather than immediate full-time return. A typical graduated plan: commence at 4 hours per day in modified duties, increase by 1 hour per week to full time, monitor for symptom recurrence at each increment, and have a clear pathway for adjusting the plan if symptoms recur.</p>

<h2>The Cost Benefit</h2>
<p>The cost of ergonomic modifications for a RTW program ($500–$3,000 in most cases) is typically 5–20% of the weekly compensation and medical costs of a musculoskeletal claim. Employers who invest proactively in ergonomic RTW support see measurably faster return to full duties, lower claim durations, and lower total claim costs — making the ergonomic investment highly cost-effective from both a workers' compensation and human perspective.</p>

<div class="cta-block">
<h3>Support Your Injured Workers' Return</h3>
<p>The Corporate Desk can supply ergonomic furniture solutions to support return-to-work programs. Contact us for rapid assessment and supply.</p>
</div>
`,
