    content: `
<p class="lead">Sedentary office work is now recognised in the health research literature as an independent risk factor for chronic disease — separate from and additive to the risks associated with insufficient leisure-time physical activity. This means that an employee who exercises regularly outside work hours still carries elevated health risk from sustained occupational sitting. Australian employers have both a WHS legal obligation and a strong business incentive to address the health consequences of prolonged sitting in the workplace.</p>

<h2>The Evidence Base</h2>
<p>Key research findings on sedentary work health risks:</p>
<ul>
<li>Adults who sit for more than 8 hours daily have a 90% greater risk of type 2 diabetes than those who sit for 4 hours or less (Katzmarzyk et al.)</li>
<li>Prolonged sitting is associated with 24% increased risk of colon cancer, 32% increased risk of endometrial cancer, and 21% increased risk of lung cancer (Biswas et al. systematic review)</li>
<li>Each hour of sitting is associated with a 22% greater risk of developing type 2 diabetes (van der Berg et al.)</li>
<li>Musculoskeletal disorders — particularly lower back pain — are significantly more prevalent in workers with sustained sedentary work patterns</li>
</ul>

<h2>The Key Point: Exercise Doesn't Cancel Sitting</h2>
<p>The most important and counterintuitive finding from sedentary behaviour research: exercise outside work hours does not offset the metabolic and cardiovascular risks of sustained daytime sitting. An employee who goes to the gym before work but sits for 7 hours during the working day maintains elevated chronic disease risk. This finding fundamentally changes the employer's health and safety obligation — exercise programs that occur outside work hours do not meet the duty of care for managing sedentary work risks.</p>

<h2>Australian WHS Implications</h2>
<p>The accumulated body of research on sedentary work risks creates an increasingly clear WHS obligation for Australian employers: sedentary work is a recognised hazard, and employers must take reasonably practicable steps to eliminate or reduce it. The WHS hierarchy of controls applied to sedentary work risk: eliminate (redesign jobs to require standing/movement), substitute (height-adjustable desks), engineer (timed posture reminders), administrative (break policies, movement culture), and PPE (anti-fatigue mats).</p>

<h2>Practical Interventions</h2>
<p>Evidence-based interventions that reduce sedentary work time:</p>
<ul>
<li>Height-adjustable desks: most directly effective, reduces sitting time by 80–90 minutes/day when properly implemented</li>
<li>Walking meetings: scheduled walking meetings replace sedentary meeting time and deliver movement benefit</li>
<li>Printer/resource placement: positioning shared equipment to require walking</li>
<li>Active break culture: leadership modelling of standing breaks reduces social barriers to movement</li>
<li>Digital reminders: sit-stand desk timers, smart watch activity prompts, or software-based reminders</li>
</ul>

<h2>The Business Case</h2>
<p>Beyond health protection, interventions that reduce sedentary work deliver direct business benefits: improved employee health reduces absenteeism and presenteeism; reduced musculoskeletal disorder rates reduce WorkCover claims and premium impacts; and the organisation's investment in employee health improves retention and recruitment outcomes. Height-adjustable desk programs that cost $800–$1,500 per employee typically recoup their cost within 12–18 months through these operational improvements.</p>

<div class="cta-block">
<h3>Address Sedentary Risk in Your Workplace</h3>
<p>The Corporate Desk supplies height-adjustable desks and ergonomic solutions to reduce the health risks of sedentary office work.</p>
</div>
`,
